ry that russia would be invading  ukraine what did you think what came to  mind  what lessons do you think  you've learned  looking at all of this  well what i have  learned what and what  um  what surprised me so  so much was that  there must have been a massive  misreading a massive misunderstanding  uh within the russian leadership  of what has happened in ukraine over the  last decade or so  it was true maybe 10 15 years ago that  ukraine  was just a little brother of russia and  and did not really have its own  fully developed national identity but  ever since 2014 everybody who has spent  time in ukraine and i have spent time in  ukraine um in in the in the months and  years since  everybody who's been there understands  that ukraine  has changed dramatically  ukraine  looks at russia now  no longer as the big brother but as the  enemy as  the power which threatens the very  existence of my country of ukraine  and i think uh the fact that that was  not  understood in moscow when the when the  decision was made to invade when when  apparently the assumption was that  russian tanks would roll into kiev and  would be welcomed by the population as a  liberating force  wow what a dramatic miscalculation that  was so  i think that one of my central  conclusion would be  uh governments are well advised  to make sure that they don't only listen  to their  own you know uh the gut feeling  uh  what i don't know how happers in putin  arrived at his conclusions but that  governments need  uh intelligence intelligent advice from  partners for parliaments  from advisory bodies and if russia had  made any use of such  of such elements of such help they would  certainly not have decided to attack  what a disastrous mistake that was  and for the west what are the lessons  learned and for the west of course uh  we must re-learn  the idea of deterrence  in a way you know deterrence by the west  of russia has failed  obviously the sanctions which we enacted  after 2014  did not  sufficiently deter russia from thinking  well  maybe we can not only do that again what  we did in 2014 maybe we can take it a  step or two further so  our deterrence effort failed and we must  rethink  how we can strengthen our own ability to  deter and to defend our interests our  borders whether this is the eu whether  it's nato  or whether it's  individual countries  the idea which was very popular in my  own country in germany for the last 30  years the idea that we are now going to  be enjoying forever the peace dividend  uh that idea is of course now  um  you know has gone out the window we uh  we need to accept the idea the fact  that war has returned  as an instrument of politics to the  european continent and that is why  defense  military capacity  and deterrence whether we like it or not  are back on the table as very important  elements of our of our own existence  my final question ambassador  what are your your thoughts about  vladimir putin  um  is  is anything that  peace negotiation would be aimed at  achieving  uh can can can people rely on that if  vladimir putin is still in charge at the  kremlin is is he the factor here  the problem of course as i see it is  that  there is no  uh is that that there is  one man rule in in moscow  uh in the in in  in the soviet days  uh they had a police bureau they had a  central committee uh  they governed by committee in if you  wish um  the putin system is is a system where  one man exactly  only one man makes the decisions and  that of course is particularly  dangerous for any country and especially  for a large and important global powers  such as such as as russia  i think that whatever  outcome  will be possible  to this war  it it will  it will need very very strong  international  guarantees  uh i think it would be foolish  to rely on verbal or written  declarations by  the russian federation  led by president putin after all the  deception  the misinformation  the disinformation  that we've  gone through in in recent months and  years  so i think  the trust  trusting russia  will actually  become possible only in a post-putin era  i would imagine  ambassador ishinga as always thanks  thank you very much for your time this  morning  thank you great talking to you i enjoyed  that very much thank you
 

